"""English — details, faith_b_1 (40 questions)."""

D = {
"faith_071": "The name comes in the second of the three verses that close Surah Al-Hashr: \"He is Allah, other than whom there is no deity, the Sovereign, the Pure, the Peace, the Bestower of security, the Overseer, the Exalted in Might, the Compeller, the Superior. Exalted is Allah above whatever they associate with Him\" (59:23). The next verse says that whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him (59:24). A Muslim who calls Allah As-Salam affirms that no fault or weakness touches Him, and seeks safety from Him alone.",
"faith_072": "Verse 59:22 opens the three closing verses of Surah Al-Hashr, right after the image of a mountain that would split from fear of Allah if the Qur'an were sent down upon it (59:21). Its first words deny every other deity; then two attributes are joined: He knows the unseen and the witnessed, so nothing hidden or visible escapes Him, and He is \"the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful\" (59:22). A Muslim learns here that the One who knows everything about him is also the One whose mercy is greatest.",
"faith_073": "The full verse reads: \"And to Allah belong the best names, so invoke Him by them. And leave those who practise deviation concerning His names. They will be recompensed for what they have been doing\" (7:180). Surah Al-Isra gives the same teaching: \"Call upon Allah or call upon the Most Merciful. Whichever you call, to Him belong the best names\" (17:110). A Muslim uses the names in supplication, calling Allah the Forgiving when he asks forgiveness and the Provider when he asks provision.",
"faith_074": "Verse 20:8 closes the opening of Surah Ta-Ha. After saying that the Qur'an is a reminder (20:2-3), the surah describes its Sender: He created the earth and the heavens, He is the Most Merciful established above the Throne, and He knows the secret and what is more hidden still (20:4-7). Then comes the summary: \"Allah — there is no deity except Him. To Him belong the best names\" (20:8). A Muslim reads the names as the description of the One who sent the Book.",
"faith_075": "The verse begins by presenting Allah as \"the Creator of the heavens and the earth\" who made mates for people and for the cattle; then it says: \"There is nothing like unto Him, and He is the Hearing, the Seeing\" (42:11). The next verse adds that to Him belong the keys of the heavens and the earth (42:12). The formula denies any likeness to Him and affirms that He hears and sees. A Muslim speaks of Allah as the Qur'an does, without comparing Him to anything created.",
"faith_076": "The Throne Verse opens with two names: \"Allah — there is no deity except Him, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of all existence\", and then says that neither drowsiness nor sleep overtakes Him (2:255). The rest of the verse shows why: all that is in the heavens and the earth is His, He knows what is before people and behind them, and preserving the heavens and the earth does not tire Him. A Muslim who recites it knows that the One watching over him never rests, day or night.",
"faith_077": "The verse reads: \"And with Him are the keys of the unseen; none knows them except Him. And He knows what is on the land and in the sea. Not a leaf falls but that He knows it. And no grain is there within the darknesses of the earth and no moist or dry thing but that it is in a clear record\" (6:59). The next verse adds that He knows what people do by day (6:60). A Muslim lives knowing that nothing he does is unknown to Allah.",
"faith_078": "The verse belongs to the opening of Surah Qaf, where Allah answers those who doubt the resurrection: He created man and knows what his soul whispers to him (50:16). The two verses that follow describe the two receiving angels seated on the right and on the left, and state: \"Man does not utter any word except that with him is an observer prepared\" (50:17-18). Even a thought that never becomes a word is known. A Muslim watches over his inner whispers and his speech, since none of them is hidden.",
"faith_079": "The verse is addressed to the believers: \"O you who have believed, protect yourselves and your families from a Fire whose fuel is people and stones, over which are angels, harsh and severe\" (66:6). The next verse turns to the disbelievers and tells them that on that Day no excuses will help them (66:7). The angels are described by their obedience: they carry out exactly what they are ordered. A Muslim takes from the verse a duty toward his household: to guard his family from the Fire, not only himself.",
"faith_080": "The two verses answer a false claim: \"And they say: The Most Merciful has taken a son. Exalted is He! Rather, they are honoured servants\" (21:26-27). The verse after them adds that He knows what is before them and behind them, that they intercede only for one He approves, and that they are apprehensive from fear of Him (21:28). The angels are servants, honoured but never sons, and their whole existence is obedience. A Muslim honours the angels as obedient servants of Allah and gives worship to Him alone.",
"faith_081": "The verse in full: \"Say: Whoever is an enemy to Jibril — it is he who has brought it down upon your heart by permission of Allah, confirming that which was before it and as guidance and good tidings for the believers\" (2:97). The word \"Say\" shows the Prophet ﷺ being told how to answer those who declared themselves enemies of Jibril. Surah Ash-Shu'ara describes the same descent: \"the Trustworthy Spirit has brought it down upon your heart\" (26:193-194). A Muslim knows that the Qur'an came through Jibril.",
"faith_082": "The verse completes the answer begun in 2:97: \"Whoever is an enemy to Allah and His angels and His messengers and Jibril and Mikal — then indeed, Allah is an enemy to the disbelievers\" (2:98). After naming the angels as a whole, it names Jibril and Mikal by themselves, so that no one could accept one and reject the other. A Muslim learns that faith in the angels is faith in all of them, and enmity toward any of them is enmity toward Allah.",
"faith_083": "The verse continues with a second list: \"And whoever disbelieves in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, and the Last Day has certainly gone far astray\" (4:136). Read together, the two halves name Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers and the Last Day. People who already believe are told to believe: the command asks them to hold firmly to what they have accepted. A Muslim learns from the verse that faith is a whole, not one item, and that the earlier Scriptures belong to it too.",
"faith_084": "The verse reads: \"And your Lord is most knowing of whoever is in the heavens and the earth. And We have made some of the prophets exceed others, and to Dawud We gave the Zabur\" (17:55). The Zabur is named again in Surah An-Nisa, among the revelations to earlier prophets (4:163), and Surah Al-Anbiya quotes from it: \"We have already written in the Zabur, after the Reminder, that the land is inherited by My righteous servants\" (21:105). A Muslim counts the Zabur among the Books sent down by Allah.",
"faith_085": "The surah opens with the Prophet ﷺ being told to report what was revealed to him about the jinn who listened (72:1). Their words follow: \"It guides to the right course, and we have believed in it. And we will never associate with our Lord anyone\" (72:2). Surah Al-Ahqaf adds that they went back to their people as warners (46:29). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an was sent to jinn as well as mankind, and that hearing it with an open heart leads to faith.",
"faith_086": "The verse is followed by two more that explain it: \"I do not want from them any provision, nor do I want them to feed Me. Indeed, it is Allah who is the Provider, the firm possessor of strength\" (51:56-58). Worship is not something Allah needs; He is the One who provides for those who worship Him. A Muslim finds in 51:56 the answer to why he exists, and sees every act of obedience as his purpose.",
"faith_087": "The verse goes on: \"So he who is drawn away from the Fire and admitted to Paradise has attained success. And what is the life of this world except the enjoyment of delusion\" (3:185). It comes in Surah Al-'Imran after verses about those who rejected the messengers, and turns the reader from this life to the next. The same words open verses in Surah Al-Anbiya (21:35) and Surah Al-'Ankabut (29:57). A Muslim remembers that death is certain for everyone and that the real reward comes on the Day of Resurrection.",
"faith_088": "Surah Az-Zalzalah first describes the earth shaken and throwing out its burdens while man asks what is happening to it; that Day the earth reports its news, because its Lord has inspired it (99:1-5). Then people come forth in separate groups to be shown their deeds (99:6), and the surah closes: \"So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it\" (99:7-8). A Muslim takes from the surah that no good is too small to matter.",
"faith_089": "The scene comes after all people are presented before their Lord in rows (18:47-48). Then: \"And the record will be placed, and you will see the criminals fearful of what is within it\" (18:49). The verse ends: \"And they will find what they did present. And your Lord does injustice to no one\" (18:49). Their cry of woe comes from the completeness of the book, not from any error. A Muslim knows that his deeds are written and the account will be just.",
"faith_090": "The verse follows Allah's words to the guilty on that Day: \"This is the Hell which you were promised\" (36:63-64). Then the mouths are sealed and the hands and feet speak instead (36:65). Surah An-Nur says the same: on a Day when their tongues, their hands and their feet will bear witness against them about what they used to do (24:24). The body that did the deeds becomes the witness to them. A Muslim learns to be careful with what his limbs do, since they will one day testify.",
"faith_091": "The words come at the end of a passage on prayer in time of danger, when the believers pray in turns while others stand guard (4:102). The verse then says: \"And when you have completed the prayer, remember Allah standing, sitting, or lying on your sides. But when you become secure, re-establish prayer\" (4:103), and closes with the decree of fixed times. Even fear of an enemy does not cancel the prayer; it only changes its form. A Muslim keeps the prayers within their times, whatever his circumstances.",
"faith_092": "The full verse is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ: \"Recite what has been revealed to you of the Book and establish prayer. Indeed, prayer prohibits immorality and wrongdoing, and the remembrance of Allah is greater. And Allah knows that which you do\" (29:45). Reciting the Qur'an and establishing prayer are joined, and the prayer is described by what it does in a person's life. A Muslim expects his prayer to show in his conduct, keeping him away from what is shameful and wrong.",
"faith_093": "The verse sits among the rulings of Surah Al-Baqarah on divorce and widows (2:234-242). The next verse continues it: \"And if you fear, then pray on foot or riding. And when you are secure, then remember Allah as He has taught you what you did not know\" (2:239). The Qur'an names the middle prayer without saying which of the five it is. A Muslim takes from 2:238 that all the prayers are to be guarded, in family life as at any other time.",
"faith_094": "These are the first words Allah speaks to Musa at the fire. He is told to remove his sandals because he is in the sacred valley of Tuwa, and that he has been chosen, so he must listen to what is revealed (20:12-13). Then: \"Indeed, I am Allah. There is no deity except Me, so worship Me and establish prayer for My remembrance\" (20:14). The first command to the new prophet, after the oneness of Allah, is the prayer. A Muslim prays to keep Allah in mind.",
"faith_095": "The surah opens with the announcement of success and then lists the believers' qualities: humble in prayer, turning away from idle talk, giving zakat, guarding their chastity, keeping trusts and promises, and maintaining their prayers (23:1-9). The list ends: \"Those are the inheritors who will inherit al-Firdaws; they will abide therein eternally\" (23:10-11). Prayer both opens and closes the list, first by its inner quality and then by its being kept up. A Muslim learns that success begins with how he stands in prayer, not only with whether he prays.",
"faith_096": "The verse sets out the prayer across the day, from the sun's decline through the darkness of the night, and then singles out the dawn (17:78). The next verse turns to the night: \"And from the night, pray with it as additional worship for you; it is expected that your Lord will resurrect you to a praised station\" (17:79). The dawn prayer is called a recitation because the Qur'an is recited in it. A Muslim gives Fajr particular care, rising for it while the world sleeps.",
"faith_097": "Verse 2:153 opens a passage on trial. Right after it, the believers are told not to call those killed in the way of Allah dead, and that they will be tested with fear, hunger and loss (2:154-155). Good tidings go to the patient, \"who, when disaster strikes them, say: Indeed we belong to Allah, and indeed to Him we will return\" (2:156). The same command appears earlier in the surah (2:45). A Muslim turns to patience and prayer in trouble.",
"faith_098": "The five daily prayers have fixed numbers of rak'ahs, known to every Muslim who prays and taught in every mosque: Fajr has two, Dhuhr four, 'Asr four, Maghrib three and 'Isha four, seventeen in all. Dhuhr is prayed at what the Qur'an calls the decline of the sun (17:78). The Qur'an allows the prayer to be shortened on a journey (4:101), and it is the four-rak'ah prayers, Dhuhr among them, that are then prayed as two. A Muslim learns each count as he learns to pray.",
"faith_099": "The five obligatory prayers follow the course of the day: Fajr before sunrise, Dhuhr after the sun passes its highest point, 'Asr in the afternoon, Maghrib just after sunset, and 'Isha once night has fallen. The Qur'an points to this span when it commands prayer \"at the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night, and the recitation at dawn\" (17:78). Any prayer timetable in a mosque shows the same five in the same order. A Muslim closes each day with 'Isha and opens the next with Fajr.",
"faith_100": "Sujud is the moment in the prayer when the worshipper places his forehead on the ground; each rak'ah contains two prostrations, with a short sitting between them. The Qur'an uses the word for the believers: \"You see them bowing and prostrating, seeking bounty from Allah and His pleasure\" (48:29), and commands: \"O you who have believed, bow and prostrate and worship your Lord\" (22:77). The last verse of Surah Al-'Alaq says: \"prostrate and draw near\" (96:19). A Muslim learns the sujud as the lowest and nearest point of his prayer.",
"faith_101": "The verse belongs to the passage that prescribes fasting (2:183-187). It describes the fast as \"a limited number of days\", gives the concession for the sick and the traveller, and adds: \"but to fast is best for you, if you only knew\" (2:184). The next verse names the month, Ramadan, repeats the same allowance word for word, and explains it: \"Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship\" (2:185). A Muslim who is ill or travelling may break the fast and make up the days later.",
"faith_102": "The verse opens the passage on fasting in Surah Al-Baqarah with the words \"O you who have believed\", and places the fast in a long line: it was prescribed for the communities before this one as well (2:183). Two verses later the month is named: Ramadan, \"in which was revealed the Qur'an, a guidance for the people\" (2:185). The stated aim is not hunger for its own sake but taqwa, awareness of Allah. A Muslim fasts to become more conscious of Allah.",
"faith_103": "The verse ends: \"So let them respond to Me and believe in Me that they may be guided\" (2:186). It stands between the verse that names Ramadan (2:185) and the verse on the nights of fasting (2:187). Unlike many verses that open with \"Say\", here the answer comes directly from Allah in the first person: \"I am near\". The nearness is shown by the response to every caller who calls on Him. A Muslim takes from this that supplication, especially in Ramadan, is heard without any intermediary.",
"faith_104": "The verse closes with the words: \"an obligation imposed by Allah. And Allah is Knowing and Wise\" (9:60). It begins with \"only\", which restricts zakat to the eight groups named and to no one else. It comes just after verses describing people who criticised the Prophet ﷺ over the distribution of charity, pleased when given a share and angry when not (9:58). By fixing the recipients Himself, Allah takes the matter out of anyone's preference. A Muslim gives zakat to those Allah has named, not wherever he likes.",
"faith_105": "The verse is addressed to the Prophet ﷺ and continues: \"and invoke blessings upon them. Indeed, your invocations are reassurance for them. And Allah is Hearing and Knowing\" (9:103). The verse before it speaks of people who confessed their sins, having mixed good deeds with bad, and holds out hope that Allah will turn to them (9:102). The two effects named, purifying and causing to grow, concern the giver, not the money. A Muslim gives zakat expecting it to cleanse him and make his good grow.",
"faith_106": "The verse comes right after believers are told to pardon and overlook the hostility of some People of the Book until Allah brings His command (2:109), and it ends: \"Indeed, Allah of what you do is Seeing\" (2:110). The pairing runs through the whole surah: \"establish prayer and give zakat and bow with those who bow\" (2:43), and those who establish prayer and give zakat have their reward with their Lord (2:277). A Muslim understands that his prayer and his zakat belong together, both good sent ahead for himself.",
"faith_107": "The verse comes in the passage on the sacrificial animals of the pilgrimage, which the previous verse calls symbols of Allah, to be shared with the needy (22:36). After saying what does not reach Allah, the verse continues: \"Thus have We subjected them to you that you may glorify Allah for that to which He has guided you; and give good tidings to the doers of good\" (22:37). A Muslim sacrifices as an act of piety and gratitude, not as an offering Allah needs.",
"faith_108": "The verse describes the pilgrimage: \"Hajj is during well-known months. So whoever has made Hajj obligatory upon himself therein, there is no sexual relations and no disobedience and no disputing during Hajj. And whatever good you do, Allah knows it\" (2:197). Then comes the command to take provisions, with the reminder of the best one, and the closing words: \"And fear Me, O you of understanding\" (2:197). Food for the road is needed, but the pilgrim's real preparation is awareness of Allah. A Muslim prepares his heart as he packs.",
"faith_109": "The verse belongs to the passage where Allah recalls settling Ibrahim at the site of the House, telling him to purify it for those who circle it, stand, bow and prostrate, and to proclaim the pilgrimage (22:26-27). Then the pilgrims are told: \"Then let them end their untidiness and fulfil their vows and perform tawaf around the Ancient House\" (22:29). Later the same House is named as the place of sacrifice (22:33). A Muslim who circles the Ka'bah walks where Ibrahim prepared it for that.",
"faith_110": "The verse begins by allowing the pilgrims to seek bounty from their Lord, and after the command to remember Allah at al-Mash'ar al-Haram it adds: \"And remember Him, as He has guided you, for indeed, you were before that among those astray\" (2:198). The next verse tells them to depart from where the people depart and ask Allah's forgiveness (2:199). A Muslim learns that Hajj is a journey of remembrance.",
}
